      "quote": "A MedDiet has been associated with improved metabolic syndrome (MetS) outcomes, including a decreased risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and obesity, among adults. However, the extent to which different levels of adherence to the MedDiet are associated with MetS outcomes in youth remains poorly understood.",
      "reason": "The introduction explicitly describes the existing knowledge (benefits in adults) and highlights the key uncertainty (unknown association in children/adolescents)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "The prevalence of metabolic disorders is increasing globally, especially among adolescents, and this has become a public health concern... Therefore, the aim of this systematic review and meta‑analysis was to investigate whether adherence to a MedDiet is associated with MetS in this population.",
      "reason": "The text explains why the review is needed, linking the growing public‑health burden to the gap in knowledge and stating the review’s purpose."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "To our knowledge, no previous systematic review or meta‑analysis has synthesised the available evidence examining the association between adherence to the MedDiet and MetS among children and adolescents.",
      "reason": "The authors state that no similar systematic reviews exist, so the conditional requirement to discuss existing reviews does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "Several potential mechanisms may explain the observed association between adherence to the MedDiet and improved MetS. The MedDiet is characterised by a high intake of fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts, seeds and healthy fats...",
      "reason": "Although mechanisms are described, the review is not of an intervention effect; therefore the conditional element concerning intervention mechanisms is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No logic model or schematic of hypothesised relationships is presented, and the review does not involve a complex intervention requiring such a model."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 92
}

